= Ocean acidification — Technical =

== Carbonate-system reactions ==
Dissolved atmospheric carbon dioxide participates in aqueous carbonate equilibria, schematically:

<pre>
CO2(aq) + H2O <=> H2CO3 <=> H+ + HCO3-
HCO3- <=> H+ + CO3(2-)
</pre>

This shorthand suppresses activity coefficients, hydration equilibria and explicit charge/species balances. Increasing dissolved inorganic carbon under relevant ocean conditions generally lowers pH and reduces carbonate-ion concentration, with effects dependent on alkalinity, temperature, salinity and mixing.

== Saturation state ==
Calcium carbonate saturation depends on the ion activity product of dissolved calcium and carbonate relative to the mineral's solubility product. Changes can affect biological calcification, but consequences vary by mineral form, organism and environment.

== Observations ==
Ocean chemistry may be constrained from pH, total alkalinity, dissolved inorganic carbon and pCO2 in combinations appropriate to the measurement setting. A numerical pH trend must specify the pH scale, calibration, sample depth and observation window.

